\date{2025-11-09T21:56:57Z}
\title{Typing rule for bind}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{The basic rule for bind (\em{aka} sequencing) in the \sf{Cmd} monad is as follows:

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swty{\Gamma}{c_1}{\Cmd{\Phi_1}{\tau_1}}{\Delta_1} \\
     \swty{\Gamma}{c_2}{\Cmd{\Phi_2}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_2}
    }
    {\swty{\Gamma}{\sequence{c_1}{c_2}}{\Cmd{\Phi_1 \cup \Phi_2}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2}}
}

This says that if #{c_1} is a command requring capabilities #{\Delta_1} to evaluate and #{\Phi_1} to execute, and which produces a value of type #{\tau_1}, and #{c_2} is a command requiring capabilities #{\Delta_2} to evaluate and #{\Phi_2} to execute, producing a value of type #{\tau_2}, then \sequence{c_1}{c_2} is a command requring capabilities #{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2} to evaluate, #{\Phi_1 \cup \Phi_2} to execute, and producing a #{\tau_2}.
}

\p{Note that this rule corresponds to a particular evaluation strategy, namely, evaluating #{c_1} and #{c_2} eagerly at the time \sequence{c_1}{c_2} is evaluated. If we instead wanted to wait to evaluate #{c_1} and #{c_2} at execution time, then we would need a rule like

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swty{\Gamma}{c_1}{\Cmd{\Phi_1}{\tau_1}}{\Delta_1} \\
     \swty{\Gamma}{c_2}{\Cmd{\Phi_2}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_2}
    }
    {\swty{\Gamma}{\sequence{c_1}{c_2}}{\Cmd{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2 \cup \Phi_1 \cup \Phi_2}{\tau_2}}{\varnothing}}
}

To see the difference, consider this example code:
\pre\verb<<<|
def f : Int -> Cmd Unit = ... end
def g : Dir -> Cmd Unit = ... end

def example = f 3; g north end
<<<
When are \code{f 3} and \code{g north} evaluated?  Under the first rule above, they must be evaluated eagerly, when the definition of \code{example} is evaluated.  Under the second rule above, they would be re-evaluated every time \code{example} is executed.}

\p{However, we can also bind the result of #{c_1} in #{c_2}, in which case we can no longer evaluate #{c_2} eagerly (unless we wanted to implement partial evaluation of open terms).  The rule for \sequence{x \bind c_1}{c_2} is therefore:
}

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swty{\Gamma}{c_1}{\Cmd{\Phi_1}{\tau_1}}{\Delta_1} \\
     \swty{\Gamma, x : \tau_1}{c_2}{\Cmd{\Phi_2}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_2}
    }
    {\swty{\Gamma}{\sequence{x \bind c_1}{c_2}}{\Cmd{\Phi_1 \cup \Phi_2 \cup \Delta_2}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_1}}
}
